FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] tests/bench_test.rs:304-330 (with tests/bench/mod.rs:43-45,313-325,660-669) — The "checkable maximum" is only a numeric comparison for three of four workloads. `check_size_supported` merely calls `ensure_workload_size_supported`; it never runs anything. `MAX_CONCAT_SIZE` is 10_000_000 while `run_concat` (mod.rs:660-669) concatenates a growing string `size` times, i.e. O(n²); at 10M that is ~10^14 character copies and cannot finish inside `DEFAULT_BUDGET` (60 s), so `run_workload("concat", 10_000_000, DEFAULT_BUDGET)` necessarily returns `BudgetExceeded` and leaves a detached CPU-burning worker. `primes` at 10M (trial division, ~10^10 operations through the interpreter) is likewise unmeasurable. The boundary test asserts `max + 1` is refused without ever demonstrating that `max` itself can be measured (only fibonacci is actually run at its max, in `edge_fibonacci_is_capped_where_f64_is_exact`). So the harness advertises a maximum it cannot deliver. Settle it by capping each workload at a size that completes inside the budget and adding a test that actually measures at `max`, or by renaming/redocumenting this as "largest reference answer we will materialize" rather than "checkable maximum".

2. [MINOR] tests/bench_test.rs:163-167 — The "singleton" coverage label is wrong and leaves the real boundary untested: the test calls `run_workload("primes", 2, ...)` expecting 1, which is "primes ≤ 2", not "primes below 2". The size-1 side of `expected_primes`'s `if size < 2 { return 0 }` (mod.rs:637-643) is never exercised, so the zero→one transition of the `primes` reference is unverified. Add `run_workload("primes", 1)` expecting 0.

3. [MINOR] tests/bench/mod.rs:477-483 — When `run_all`'s shared budget is exhausted (`remaining.is_zero()`), it builds `BenchError::BudgetExceeded { budget }` with the *full* budget passed to `run_all`, not the zero remaining time it just measured. The Display then claims the workload "did not finish within its 60.000s budget" when that workload was never given 60 s. Report the remaining duration (or a distinct exhausted-budget message) so the error is accurate.

4. [MINOR] BENCH.md:7-10 — The checked-in baseline records volatile, machine-specific timings and is regenerated by every `rb-bench` run. The gate never asserts them, and the uncommitted diff already shows all four changing (e.g. primes 75.685 → 81.838 ms), so every regeneration produces a meaningless diff and the "baseline" is not reproducible. Consider committing the answer column only, or writing timings to an untracked artifact.

5. [MINOR] tests/bench/mod.rs:463-466 — `bench_md_path()` resolves to the compile-time `CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR`, so the shipped `rb-bench` binary writes `BENCH.md` into the build-time source directory (read-only or gone after `cargo install`). Tests only exercise `run_cli_with_path`, so the real path is never tested and the installed binary's behaviour is unverified. Use the current working directory or an explicit env override.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 5
